Use Postgres full-text search, not a separate search service — 38k rows is trivial for it. Add a generated `tsvector` column combining `name` and `description` (weight name higher), index it with GIN, and query with `websearch_to_tsquery` + `ts_rank` for relevance ordering, reusing the existing `db.query`/pg pool pattern in routes/products.js.

```sql
ALTER TABLE products ADD COLUMN search_vector tsvector
  GENERATED ALWAYS AS (
    setweight(to_tsvector('english', name), 'A') ||
    setweight(to_tsvector('english', description), 'B')
  ) STORED;
CREATE INDEX products_search_idx ON products USING GIN (search_vector);
```

The one real fork: plain `tsvector` matching only handles whole-word/stem matches, so a typo or partial word ("wodget") returns nothing. If the product names are short/branded and typos matter, add `pg_trgm` with a GIN trigram index on `name` and blend it in (or fall back to trigram similarity when FTS returns zero rows) — more index maintenance for better fuzzy recall. If exact/stemmed matching is good enough, skip trigram and this is a single migration plus one new route (`GET /products?q=...`) filtering on `search_vector @@ websearch_to_tsquery(...) AND active`.

