Both routes trust `req.params.id` alone, so any authenticated user can act on rows belonging to another account. `req.user.accountId` is already populated by the `sessionUser` middleware (auth.js:11) on every request, so the fix is to add it to the `WHERE` clause and check that a row was actually affected.

**routes/accounts.js** — the account's own `id` must equal the caller's `accountId`, not just the URL param:

```js
router.patch("/:id", async (req, res) => {
  const rows = await db.write(
    "UPDATE accounts SET display_name = $1, billing_email = $2 WHERE id = $3 AND id = $4 RETURNING *",
    [req.body.display_name, req.body.billing_email, req.params.id, req.user.accountId]
  );
  if (!rows.length) return res.status(404).end();
  res.json(rows[0]);
});

router.delete("/:id", async (req, res) => {
  const rows = await db.write(
    "UPDATE accounts SET deleted_at = now() WHERE id = $1 AND id = $2 RETURNING id",
    [req.params.id, req.user.accountId]
  );
  if (!rows.length) return res.status(404).end();
  res.status(204).end();
});
```

**routes/orders.js** — orders have a separate `account_id` column, so scope on that:

```js
router.patch("/:id/cancel", async (req, res) => {
  const rows = await db.write(
    "UPDATE orders SET status = 'cancelled' WHERE id = $1 AND account_id = $2 RETURNING *",
    [req.params.id, req.user.accountId]
  );
  if (!rows.length) return res.status(404).end();
  res.json(rows[0]);
});
```

Same pattern for any other `:id`-based read/update/delete route in these files — add `account_id = $n` (or `id = accountId` for the accounts table itself) to the `WHERE` clause, and return 404 when the update affects zero rows so a cross-account request can't be distinguished from a not-found one.

