Direct-to-S3: the client asks the API for a signed PUT URL, uploads the photo bytes straight to the bucket, then tells the API the key to store. That's the fork the nginx config forces — `client_max_body_size 1m` is global and the payments team already declined a per-location override for PCI reasons, so a phone photo (routinely 3-8MB+) can't be proxied through `/` to `storefront`. Uploading through the app tier isn't an option here without reopening that PCI conversation.

`storage.js` already has everything for this: `signedUrl` takes `method: 'PUT'`, and CORS on the bucket already allows PUT from `market.example.com`. So the new endpoint is symmetric with the existing `/invoice` read path — mirror it as a write:

```js
router.post('/:id/photos', async (req, res) => {
  const key = `listings/${req.params.id}/${crypto.randomUUID()}`;
  res.json({ key, url: await storage.signedUrl(key, { method: 'PUT' }) });
});

router.post('/:id/photos/:key/confirm', async (req, res) => {
  await db.addPhotoKey(req.params.id, req.params.key);
  res.sendStatus(204);
});
```

Client requests the URL, `PUT`s the file straight to S3 (bypassing nginx and its body-size limit entirely), then confirms so `photo_keys` gets appended — the column's already there, just never populated. Depth left out: content-type/extension validation on the presign step, and a decision on whether confirm should HEAD the object to check it actually landed before writing the key.

