No — the log shows a leak, not a capacity shortage: idle is `0` at every sample while `waiting` climbs steadily (7→11→14→19→23→28). If this were just undersized, you'd see idle connections churn; instead none ever come free.

The cause is in `db.js:11-16`: `withClient` calls `client.release()` only after `fn(client)` returns. If `fn` throws, `release()` is skipped and that connection is checked out forever. Raising `max` would buy time but the pool would still fill up again as more errors leak clients.

Fix: wrap in try/finally so the client always releases:

```js
async function withClient(fn) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    return await fn(client);
  } finally {
    client.release();
  }
}
```
